.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "to-regex-range [![Donate](https://img.shields.io/badge/Donate-PayPal-green.svg)](https://www.paypal.com/cgi-bin/webscr?cmd=_s-xclick&hosted_button_id=W8YFZ425KND68) [![NPM version](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/to-regex-range.svg?style=flat)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/to-regex-range) [![NPM monthly downloads](https://img.shields.io/npm/dm/to-regex-range.svg?style=flat)](https://npmjs.org/package/to-regex-range) [![NPM total downloads](https://img.shields.io/npm/dt/to-regex-range.svg?style=flat)](https://npmjs.org/package/to-regex-range) [![Linux Build Status](https://img.shields.io/travis/micromatch/to-regex-range.svg?style=flat&label=Travis)](https://travis-ci.org/micromatch/to-regex-range)"
.PP
.RS 4
Pass two numbers, get a regex-compatible source string for matching ranges\&. Validated against more than 2\&.78 million test assertions\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Please consider following this project's author, \fRJon Schlinkert\fP, and consider starring the project to show your :heart: and support\&.
.SH "Install"
.PP
Install with \fRnpm\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install \-\-save to\-regex\-range
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fB\fBWhat does this do?\fP\fP
.PP
.PP
.RS 4


.PP

.br

.PP
This libary generates the \fRsource\fP string to be passed to \fRnew RegExp()\fP for matching a range of numbers\&.

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const toRegexRange = require('to\-regex\-range');
const regex = new RegExp(toRegexRange('15', '95'));
.fi
.PP

.PP
A string is returned so that you can do whatever you need with it before passing it to \fRnew RegExp()\fP (like adding \fR^\fP or \fR$\fP boundaries, defining flags, or combining it another string)\&.

.PP

.br

.PP
.RE
.PP

.PP
\fB\fBWhy use this library?\fP\fP
.PP
.PP
.RS 4


.PP

.br

.PP
.RE
.PP
.SS "Convenience"
Creating regular expressions for matching numbers gets deceptively complicated pretty fast\&.

.PP
For example, let's say you need a validation regex for matching part of a user-id, postal code, social security number, tax id, etc:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex for matching \fR1\fP => \fR/1/\fP (easy enough)
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex for matching \fR1\fP through \fR5\fP => \fR/[1-5]/\fP (not bad\&.\&.\&.)
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex for matching \fR1\fP or \fR5\fP => \fR/(1|5)/\fP (still easy\&.\&.\&.)
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex for matching \fR1\fP through \fR50\fP => \fR/([1-9]|[1-4][0-9]|50)/\fP (uh-oh\&.\&.\&.)
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex for matching \fR1\fP through \fR55\fP => \fR/([1-9]|[1-4][0-9]|5[0-5])/\fP (no prob, I can do this\&.\&.\&.)
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex for matching \fR1\fP through \fR555\fP => \fR/([1-9]|[1-9][0-9]|[1-4][0-9]{2}|5[0-4][0-9]|55[0-5])/\fP (maybe not\&.\&.\&.)
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex for matching \fR0001\fP through \fR5555\fP => \fR/(0{3}[1-9]|0{2}[1-9][0-9]|0[1-9][0-9]{2}|[1-4][0-9]{3}|5[0-4][0-9]{2}|55[0-4][0-9]|555[0-5])/\fP (okay, I get the point!)
.PP

.PP
The numbers are contrived, but they're also really basic\&. In the real world you might need to generate a regex on-the-fly for validation\&.

.PP
\fBLearn more\fP

.PP
If you're interested in learning more about \fRcharacter classes\fP and other regex features, I personally have always found \fRregular-expressions\&.info\fP to be pretty useful\&.
.SS "Heavily tested"
As of April 07, 2019, this library runs \fR>1m test assertions\fP against generated regex-ranges to provide brute-force verification that results are correct\&.

.PP
Tests run in ~280ms on my MacBook Pro, 2\&.5 GHz Intel Core i7\&.
.SS "Optimized"
Generated regular expressions are optimized:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
duplicate sequences and character classes are reduced using quantifiers
.IP "\(bu" 2
smart enough to use \fR?\fP conditionals when number(s) or range(s) can be positive or negative
.IP "\(bu" 2
uses fragment caching to avoid processing the same exact string more than once
.PP

.PP

.br
.SH "Usage"
.PP
Add this library to your javascript application with the following line of code

.PP
.PP
.nf
const toRegexRange = require('to\-regex\-range');
.fi
.PP

.PP
The main export is a function that takes two integers: the \fRmin\fP value and \fRmax\fP value (formatted as strings or numbers)\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const source = toRegexRange('15', '95');
//=> 1[5\-9]|[2\-8][0\-9]|9[0\-5]

const regex = new RegExp(`^${source}$`);
console\&.log(regex\&.test('14')); //=> false
console\&.log(regex\&.test('50')); //=> true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('94')); //=> true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('96')); //=> false
.fi
.PP
.SH "Options"
.PP
.SS "options\&.capture"
\fBType\fP: \fRboolean\fP

.PP
\fBDeafault\fP: \fRundefined\fP

.PP
Wrap the returned value in parentheses when there is more than one regex condition\&. Useful when you're dynamically generating ranges\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(toRegexRange('\-10', '10'));
//=> \-[1\-9]|\-?10|[0\-9]

console\&.log(toRegexRange('\-10', '10', { capture: true }));
//=> (\-[1\-9]|\-?10|[0\-9])
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.shorthand"
\fBType\fP: \fRboolean\fP

.PP
\fBDeafault\fP: \fRundefined\fP

.PP
Use the regex shorthand for \fR[0-9]\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(toRegexRange('0', '999999'));
//=> [0\-9]|[1\-9][0\-9]{1,5}

console\&.log(toRegexRange('0', '999999', { shorthand: true }));
//=> \\d|[1\-9]\\d{1,5}
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.relaxZeros"
\fBType\fP: \fRboolean\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRtrue\fP

.PP
This option relaxes matching for leading zeros when when ranges are zero-padded\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const source = toRegexRange('\-0010', '0010');
const regex = new RegExp(`^${source}$`);
console\&.log(regex\&.test('\-10')); //=> true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('\-010')); //=> true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('\-0010')); //=> true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('10')); //=> true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('010')); //=> true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('0010')); //=> true
.fi
.PP

.PP
When \fRrelaxZeros\fP is false, matching is strict:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const source = toRegexRange('\-0010', '0010', { relaxZeros: false });
const regex = new RegExp(`^${source}$`);
console\&.log(regex\&.test('\-10')); //=> false
console\&.log(regex\&.test('\-010')); //=> false
console\&.log(regex\&.test('\-0010')); //=> true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('10')); //=> false
console\&.log(regex\&.test('010')); //=> false
console\&.log(regex\&.test('0010')); //=> true
.fi
.PP
.SH "Examples"
.PP
